## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Insights

The evidence gathered supports all three core assertions in Joe Biden's October 30, 2020 claim about Donald Trump. First, Trump did call climate change a "hoax" on multiple occasions, most notably in a 2012 tweet, and continued to do so for years afterward (though he later partially walked this back in 2018). Second, Trump did suggest using nuclear weapons to disrupt hurricanes during official White House briefings, with Axios reporting his specific suggestion of dropping a bomb "inside the eye of the hurricane" as it moves across the Atlantic—a proposal he reportedly raised "multiple times" and that was documented in a 2017 NSC memo. Third, Trump did make the now-infamous Revolutionary War airports comment during his July 4, 2019 "Salute to America" speech, saying troops "took over the airports," and he has repeatedly referred to himself as a "stable genius." Biden's paraphrasing of the airports remark as "the biggest problem we had in the Revolutionary War is we didn't have enough airports" is a comedic distillation of Trump's actual gaffe but accurately captures its substance.

## Missing Information

No significant information is lacking. All three substantive claims have been verified through multiple credible sources (PBS, Axios, Snopes, Business Insider, USA Today), and the "stable genius" self-description is well-documented. The evidence is sufficient to reach a clear conclusion.

## Conclusion

The evidence clearly and consistently supports each of the three main assertions in Biden's claim: Trump calling climate change a "hoax," Trump suggesting detonating a nuclear bomb over the Atlantic to disrupt hurricanes, and Trump's Revolutionary War airports gaffe. The "stable genius" self-reference is also well-documented. Therefore, my decision is `supported`.

